<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ADR-009 D7 (phase O5): the bundled mono family, and the end of a measured defect.

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. Every mono style in this app rendered `monospace`, which on Android is Droid
  Sans Mono. That family does not cover U+2500..257F, so the terminal peek's frame resolved through
  font FALLBACK to glyphs in another family at 0.71em against the mono family's own 0.60em: the
  frame drew, 18 percent wider per character than the text it framed, and did not line up.
  MonoBoxDrawingTest measured that, ADR-007 B134 recorded it, and D7 is the decision that pays for
  the fix.

  TWO FACES AND NOT THREE. The type scale asks the mono family for 400, 500 and 600. The two below
  answer 400 and 500 exactly; 600 resolves to the nearest, which is the 500 here, and Android
  applies no synthetic bolding for a 100 unit gap. Every glyph in both faces advances 600/1000 em,
  including the box drawing block, so nothing about the frame's grid depends on which weight a
  style asked for. A third static face would be another 273 KB for a weight difference at 10 sp.

  FEATURES ARE NOT DECLARED HERE. `tnum`, `zero` and `calt` are per style, in res/values/type.xml,
  because a family cannot state them and because the decision is about WHERE machine data renders
  rather than about the face.

  The two file names are the resource spelling of the release's own: JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf and
  JetBrainsMono-Medium.ttf, v2.304, OFL-1.1. The licence and the authors file travel with them at
  docs/design/fonts/, which is OFL-1.1's own condition and not a courtesy.
-->
<font-family xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <font
        android:font="@font/jetbrains_mono_regular"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="400" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/jetbrains_mono_medium"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="500" />
</font-family>
